so you can get a quick overview as to how the project looks.

192
00:19:19,886 --> 00:19:30,576
The first file is our ViteConfig.ts, which is essentially just a configuration file for Vite, which is the underlying bundler tool we're using to build and serve our application.

193
00:19:30,958 --> 00:19:36,708
Now, in the past, if you've used other apps like Webpack or Next.js, you'll have a Nextconfig.

194
00:19:37,006 --> 00:19:42,980
This is the equivalent to that, where you can define all the functionality you want for the build in Dev Server.

195
00:19:43,438 --> 00:19:53,764
So here we're setting up a configuration with define config from Vite that allows us to extend the Vite configuration by adding additional plugins and functionality.

196
00:19:54,176 --> 00:20:05,796
So here we're using default plugins for Tanstack out of the box, including the DevTools here, which is the DevTools bubble we just saw that allows us to interact with our DevTools.

197
00:20:07,118 --> 00:20:14,116
We also have the Tanstack router itself being set up here, specifying a target, as well as automatic code splitting.

198
00:20:15,534 --> 00:20:20,420
Which is an advanced optimization feature, which is nice that they offer this out of the box.

199
00:20:20,878 --> 00:20:25,316
We also have a Vite React configuration and configuration for Tailwind.

200
00:20:25,774 --> 00:20:34,576
And so this is a really nice benefit of Vite and TanStack is that it sort of abstracts a lot of this configuration away into simple functions that we can just call,

201
00:20:34,816 --> 00:20:36,976
include this, and that's all we have to do.

202
00:20:37,262 --> 00:20:44,516
There's no other configuration that we have to really mess with. It's all just out of the box included, which is a really nice developer experience.

203
00:20:45,230 --> 00:20:57,056
And then finally, we also have a path alias set up here, which just allows us to import using the at symbol, and this will point directly at our source directory for any code that we want to import.

204
00:20:59,278 --> 00:21:05,220
From here, we just have a familiar TS config.json to configure our TypeScript compilation options.

205
00:21:06,126 --> 00:21:08,260
These defaults out of the box will work great.

206
00:21:08,814 --> 00:21:11,492
We also have the package JSON that we just took a look at.

207
00:21:12,270 --> 00:21:16,496
We have a starting index HTML, which is the bootstrap of our application.

208
00:21:16,576 --> 00:21:27,172
This is where we define any initial loading scripts, the title of the app, and finally, this is the root div where we actually bootstrap the app. We have the

209
00:21:27,822 --> 00:21:32,256
important ID app div here where the JavaScript of the app actually hooks into.

210
00:21:32,590 --> 00:21:41,540
And then finally, we have the script where we're actually requiring our main.tsx, and that's what we'll look at next. That's the entry point to the Tanstack app.

211
00:21:41,998 --> 00:21:45,412
Next up, you'll see we have this.ca.json file.

212
00:21:45,806 --> 00:21:53,540
And so this is a Tanstack tooling artifact that's used by Tanstack Router for code generation and analysis.

213
00:21:54,096 --> 00:22:02,820
So one of the major benefits of Tanstack Router is a complete type safety of all the router operations that we're going to use in the app.

214
00:22:03,118 --> 00:22:13,536
So whenever we navigate to routes, use query parameters or path parameters, this is all going to be completely type safe because we're actually turning our route files into

