00:29:40,174 --> 00:29:46,724
and also show you how you can integrate Tanstack Query to directly reach out to a backend like Nest.js.

296
00:29:47,310 --> 00:29:51,588
And so for us, this default configuration for the route is just what we want.

297
00:29:51,918 --> 00:29:58,656
And so here we're defining the app component, and this is a simple React component. It's just basic JSX that we now provide.

298
00:29:58,894 --> 00:30:04,336
And that means whenever we go to this route, we're going to render this JSX. And so it's as simple as that.

299
00:30:04,526 --> 00:30:08,132
I think it's a very nice declarative way to define our routes

300
00:30:08,910 --> 00:30:14,084
because we keep the route definition and the component in the same file. It's really nice and clean to me.

301
00:30:14,766 --> 00:30:24,096
And notice another amazing benefit to using Tanstack Router and Tanstack Start over Next.js is you're never ever going to have to think about writing use

302
00:30:24,336 --> 00:30:25,536
client anymore, right?

303
00:30:25,806 --> 00:30:36,016
Notice we don't have to have any use client directive in here. There's no need for it anymore because Tanstack Router doesn't use server components at all.

304
00:30:36,430 --> 00:30:40,004
Tanstack Router is client focused, not server focused.

305
00:30:40,558 --> 00:30:49,924
In my opinion, it's a much better developer experience. You're not going to have to think about client or server components or run into any annoying errors about this anymore.

306
00:30:50,158 --> 00:30:54,336
We're just going to focus on writing JSX and our components, okay?

307
00:30:54,606 --> 00:30:56,324
Everything is client focused.

308
00:30:56,814 --> 00:30:59,908
Now you might be concerned about the performance implications of this.

309
00:31:00,462 --> 00:31:03,908
And like I said before, there are ways to actually

310
00:31:05,198 --> 00:31:13,936
still preload this JSX on the Tanstack server side using Tanstack Loader functions, which run before rendering.

311
00:31:14,286 --> 00:31:19,780
They can run on the client or the server, but they're not components, they're separate server-side functions.

312
00:31:20,398 --> 00:31:25,296
And I want to make that point, very clear: is if you're just using Tanstack router

313
00:31:26,062 --> 00:31:31,396
as a client-facing application, which I think is a great move for most apps.

314
00:31:31,662 --> 00:31:34,736
and you just want to call a already existing backend

315
00:31:35,470 --> 00:31:39,588
using a tool like Tanstack Query, which is exactly what we're going to learn in this lecture.

316
00:31:39,822 --> 00:31:41,776
then you don't have to worry about any of this.

317
00:31:41,934 --> 00:31:44,656
We can just focus on our client facing code.

318
00:31:44,974 --> 00:31:48,644
Tanstack router will still be very performant, very fast.

319
00:31:48,942 --> 00:31:55,044
The developer experience is amazing, and we're going to see how we can reach out to our backend directly with Tanstack Query.

320
00:31:55,566 --> 00:32:04,480
So I just wanted to drop this note because I think this is a great benefit, is not having to worry about these server components and marking every component as use

321
00:32:04,640 --> 00:32:07,940
client, which I just think is really obnoxious in the next GS world.

322
00:32:08,240 --> 00:32:12,160
Now everything is client focused and is a great developer experience.

